# Sensitive Data in Logs

## Choose safe metadata at the call site

Use explicit, minimal metadata such as counts, durations, status codes, and
booleans. Follow the project's existing logging framework and event structure.

Inspect every field passed to a log or telemetry call. Do not assume an object
is safe because it is a request, response, configuration, or error object.
Avoid dumping whole payloads when they may contain sensitive fields.

## Exclude sensitive values

Keep credentials, authentication headers, access tokens, signing keys, passwords,
and private personal identifiers out of logs. Signed URLs and their query
strings can contain credentials and must not be logged in full.

This applies equally to structured fields, free-text messages, exception text,
console output, and debug statements. Check error paths as well as success paths.
Use the project's data classification to decide whether an identifier is safe;
do not assume every operational identifier is public.

Existing redaction is defense in depth, not a reason to pass unnecessary
sensitive data to the logger. Check the actual logging pipeline before claiming
that it sanitizes a value.
